        "**Q3.**\n",
        "1. Find a dataset on a topic you're interested in. Some easy options are data.gov, kaggle.com, and data.world.\n",
        "2. Clean the data and do some exploratory data analysis on key variables that interest you. Pick a particular target/outcome variable and features/predictors.\n",
        "3. Split the sample into an ~80% training set and a ~20% test set.\n",
        "4. Run a few regressions of your target/outcome variable on a variety of features/predictors. Compute the RMSE on the test set.\n",
        "- **RMSE with intercept: 0.465, RMSE without intercept: 0.465**\n",
        "5. Which model performed the best, and why?\n",
        "- **Interestingly enough, the R² and RMSE values for both intercept and without intercept are very similar. This could be because of the data set, where there was only 100 rows.**\n",
        "6. What did you learn?\n",
        "- **From the previous parts, it may be best to have both categorical variables and numerical variables as predictors. Seen in the cars data set, the R² was significantly higher for the combined model rather than the model with either numerical or categorical variables only.**"
      ]
